      "text": "First, let us determine the possible values of each digit (units, tens, hundreds, thousands) of a four digit odd number and a multiple of 5, while also determining the restrictions that must be considered in order to ensure these numbers can be reached initially and subsequently.\nAny number $N$ has four distinct digits, such $N$$$=$ $\\underline{abcd}$.  A three-digit number is of the form $3a b c 3b . . . . . .$.  So in order so solve this equation, we need to get the leading digit, b, at 01,03,05,07,09,11,13,.... For example, in order for N=2,451 to be valid with four digits, we must have b=1,3,5 only.  Similarly also, we need each of b, c, d to not have a leading zero, as 420 would be a two digit number, thus not a digit in $N=$ $\\underline{abcd}$.  In our case when we are calculating A, we crucially disregard b inequality.  We will do so similarly by finding the general solution such that the constraints stated above are not fulfilled.  For these values of b, b,c, d will always have a leading zero if $x$ is even. That is, $x=2,4, 6, \\cdots$.\nThus we can obtain A by summing the four-digit numbers those satisfy $n=$ $\\underline{abcd}$ with b even.  This is equivalent to $n=$ $\\underline{abc0}$ with c and d even. By trial and error, we know that $0\\leq a\\leq 7$, $0\\leq b \\leq 4$, and $0\\leq c, d \\leq 9$.  Additionally, the constraint $d = 1b=2$ is unnecessary, it is satisfied automatically (due to a>0).  Also, the constraints $a\\neq0$ and $b\\neq0$ must be satisfied.  We are left with 8 possibilities for each digit in $n=$ $\\underline{abc0}$, $n=$ $\\underline{abc0}$ such that $1 \\leq a, b, c \\leq 9$.  The remaining possibilities for $a$ is $9$.  Thus the number of (difference case) A is:\n$$\\sum_{a\\leq b \\leq c \\leq d}^{a,b,c,d \\in [1,9]} (c+10) + (0 \\leq a \\leq 7, c+10, d+10) $$\n$$=(c+10)(11)+7a\\geq121$$\nSimilarly, for B, we simply multiply the product, $1~\\underline{000',000}$.  Thus the number of B is: $$\\sum _{n=01}^{\\infty} (c+10) + (0 \\leq a \\leq 7, c +10, d+10)'$$\n$$(c+10) =  11,21,31,41,51,61 \\cdots \\color{teal green}70,$$\nThus A+B:$$\\sum_{a\\leq b \\leq c \\leq d}^{a,b,c,d \\in [1,9]} (c+10) + (0 \\leq a \\leq 7, a,b,c,d \\in [1,9]} + (c+10) + (0 \\leq d, c+10, d \\in [1,9])$$\n$$=(c+10)(11)+7a+c+10) + (d+10)(11)+7d=(121) + 25c+ a + d = \\boxed{1460}$$\n$\\blacksquare$",
